{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And a man who takes his brother's wife — it is niddah.</b> The intent is not actual niddah with an issue of blood, but in the manner of the expression ״זמה היא״ — 'it is depravity' — and likewise ״חסד הוא״ — 'it is an unfailing loyalty' (i.e., a distorted kindness) — to say that this lying with a brother's wife is niddah, that is, she is repulsive like a niddah. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted ״נדה היא״ to prohibit partial relations (he'arah) with her, as with a zavah, for partial relations are explicitly stated concerning her, as it is said (Leviticus 20): ״את מקורה הערה״." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> ״נדה היא״ — just as niddah has a permitted time for release, so too a brother's wife has a permitted time for release (i.e., levirate marriage). Now, all the forbidden relations that were mentioned in this section he had already mentioned above in the section of אחרי מות, but there he mentioned them by way of a negative prohibition and did not mention the punishment at all, while here he returned and repeated them in order to mention the punishment. And he mentioned all of them except for two sisters, for the honor of Jacob. <br>And as for what it says <b>they shall be childless (ערירים יהיו)</b>, whereas the verse above says ״ערירים ימותו״ — 'they shall die childless' — the explanation is: if he has children, he buries them — this is ״ערירים ימותו״; and if he has no children, he dies without children — this is ״ערירים יהיו״."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:26.521685602Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}